 to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"])]), ('T-011', [(92, ['NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion.'])]), ('T-012', [(73, [\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\", 'Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.']), (122, ['NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.', 'Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.'])]), ('T-013', [(35, [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"])]), ('T-033', [(22, ['Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion,'])]), ('T-041', [(114, [\"According to the latest prints, Nvidia's quarterly data center revenue is now $89 billion -- up 117% year over year.\"]), (116, [\"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\", \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"])]), ('T-042', [(13, [\"That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"])]), ('T-043', [(13, [\"That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"])]), ('T-045', [(39, [\"Nvidia's latest quarter puts the scale of the contest in perspective: data centers generated $89 billion of its $96.2 billion in revenue, or roughly 92.5%.\"])]), ('T-046', [(39, [\"Nvidia's latest quarter puts the scale of the contest in perspective: data centers generated $89 billion of its $96.2 billion in revenue, or roughly 92.5%.\"])]), ('T-056', [(74, ['NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) generated $96.2 billion of quarterly revenue, up 106%, with Data Center revenue rising 117% to $89.0 billion and gross margin at 75%.'])]), ('T-067', [(95, ['NVIDIA (NVDA) posted $96.2B revenue; data center sales were $89B, about 92.5% of total — key revenue driver for the quarter.'])]), ('T-068', [(95, ['NVIDIA (NVDA) posted $96.2B revenue; data center sales were $89B, about 92.5% of total — key revenue driver for the quarter.'])])]\nANSWER Revenue $178 billion (company-wide; up 96% year over year) | First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026) | NVIDIA, reported (section: Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual); source as stored: Nvidia) | None \n [('T-029', [(105, ['For now, Nvidia remains primarily focused on AI. In the first half of its fiscal 2027 (which ended July 26), its revenue rose by 96% year over year to $178 billion. Also, due to the relatively slower growth in its costs and expenses, its net income surged by 161% to $118 billion.'])])]\nANSWER Revenue $215.9 billion (T-037 stores 'close to $215.9 billion'); up roughly 65.5% year over year; company total (the last full fiscal year reported) | fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026) in T-001; stored as 'Fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026' in T-037, T-038 | NVIDIA, reported (section: Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual); source as stored: NVIDIA / Nvidia) | None \n [('T-001', [(116, ['In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.'])]), ('T-037', [(229, ['In the fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a massive increase of approximately 65.5% year over year. Net income for the period was nearly $120.1 billion, producing a net margin of roughly 55.6%.'])]), ('T-038', [(229, ['In the fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a massive increase of approximately 65.5% year over year. Net income for the period was nearly $120.1 billion, producing a net margin of roughly 55.6%.'])])]\n\n S2\nANSWER Revenue growth about 70% (stored as 70%, about 70%, roughly 70%, approximately 70%); company-wide, preliminary, described as supply-constrained; made with the fiscal 2027 second-quarter results, first time NVIDIA guided a full year ahead; T-099 adds growth could double if commodity supply constraints were overcome. A growth rate only: no dollar level is stored | fiscal 2028 ('Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)' in T-095) | NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / Nvidia / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / NVIDIA management (CEO) in T-094's article 98; said late August 2026) | None \n [('T-093', [(16, ['Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.']), (201, ['the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028)'])]), ('T-094', [(43, ['Last month, Nvidia posted a market-beating Q2 and issued bullish guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which makes its current P/E multiple of about 24x appear rather attractive.']), (92, ['NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.']), (98, ['For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.']), (177, ['Management’s preliminary outlook is for revenue to grow about 70% in fiscal 2028. Management called that outlook supply-constrained. Demand without that limit would be a lot higher, management said.'])]), ('T-095', [(121, ['And on the company\\'s late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.']), (222, ['The lower multiple comes despite Nvidia forecasting roughly 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 as demand for AI training and inference continues to drive its business.'])]), ('T-096', [(54, ['Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue was $96.22 billion, up 105.8% year over year, with a Q3 guide of $108 billion at the midpoint and a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%.', \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year to $96B, making the director's trim a portfolio move, not a business warning.\"])]), ('T-097', [(22, ['while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled'])]), ('T-098', [(53, [\"On the company's earnings call in late August, chief financial officer Colette Kress went a whole year further.\", '\"We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\" Kress said. \"This is a supply-constrained outlook.\"'])]), ('T-099', [(45, ['In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips.', \"But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware.\", \"On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 2027 earnings call , Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said:\", 'Incredibly, we are seeing demand acceleration even at our scale. Customer forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply-constrained.'])])]\nANSWER Revenue growth 70% (stored as 70%, roughly 70%, around 70%), the same value stored under relative or missing period labels | stored relatively: 'next fiscal year' (T-105, T-106); 'next year' (T-108); 'next year (2027)', no fiscal/calendar basis specified (T-109); period not stored (T-113) | NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / Nvidia / NVIDIA management; T-113's basis: Nvidia forecast as described by Morningstar) | None \n [('T-105', [(55, ['management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year'])]), ('T-106', [(166, ['This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.'])]), ('T-108', [(162, ['Nvidia projects this supply/demand situation will continue, contributing to a 70% revenue growth forecast for next year.'])]), ('T-109', [(158, [\"Next year, its management team believes the company will grow revenue at a 70% pace. If you've followed Nvidia throughout the AI race, you know that management often sandbags its guidance, so the real growth rate is likely higher than this.\"])]), ('T-113', [(32, ['Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.'])])]\nANSWER Chip sales: double (NVIDIA chip sales; forecast attributed to CEO Jensen Huang) | next year | Jensen Huang, as the stored source; the section stores it as someone else's own outlook (guidance), while the line's basis calls him NVIDIA's CEO | None \n [('T-121', [(99, ['Huang, who said that Nvidia would double its chip sales next year'])])]\nANSWER Consensus sales estimate $671.95 billion (up 65.5% on the current year) | Next fiscal year | Zacks C